 Given a monic polynomial \( P(x) = x^n + a_{n-1}x^{n-1} + \cdots + a_0 \) with real coefficients, we know that \( a_{n-1} = -a_{n-2} \). We need to find the greatest lower bound for the sum of the squares of the roots of the polynomial. By Vieta's formulas, we have: \[
\text{sum of the roots} = -a_{n-1}
\]
and \[
\text{sum of the product of the roots taken two at a time} = a_{n-2}
\] The sum of the squares of the roots \( r_1, r_2, \ldots, r_n \) is given by: \[
\sum_{i=1}^{n} r_i^2 = \left(\sum_{i=1}^{n} r_i\right)^2 - 2 \sum_{1 \leq i < j \leq n} r_i r_j = (-a_{n-1})^2 - 2a_{n-2}
\] Since \( a_{n-1} = -a_{n-2} \), we substitute \( a_{n-2} \) by \( -a_{n-1} \): \[
\sum_{i=1}^{n} r_i^2 = (-a_{n-1})^2 - 2(-a_{n-1}) = a_{n-1}^2 + 2a_{n-1}
\] This is a quadratic expression in \( a_{n-1} \): \[
a_{n-1}^2 + 2a_{n-1} = (a_{n-1} + 1)^2 - 1
\] The minimum value of this quadratic expression occurs when \( a_{n-1} = -1 \), and in that case, the minimum value is \(-1\). Therefore, the greatest lower bound for the sum of the squares of the roots is \(-1\). </think> <answer>1</answer>
